"""Context-independent option facts, paired with an item-specific review rationale.
Order matters: specialized capabilities must precede broad service names.
"""
import re
FACTS=[]
def fact(pattern,text): FACTS.append((re.compile(pattern,re.I),text))
fact(r'without using credentials','Role-based access still uses temporary security credentials; it avoids distributing long-lived credentials, not all credentials.')
fact(r'Persistent credentials','Persistent access keys are long-lived credentials; an assumed role instead uses temporary credentials.')
fact(r'Shield Advanced','Shield Advanced is an optional paid DDoS tier; baseline built-in protection is Shield Standard.')
fact(r'Shield|DDoS','Shield addresses distributed denial-of-service attacks, not general IAM authorization, key management or every application exploit.')
fact(r'Secrets Manager|rotate secrets|passwords and API keys','Secrets Manager stores protected secret values and supports configured secret rotation; it does not replace network or application authorization.')
fact(r'Parameter Store|configuration data and secrets','Parameter Store stores configuration and optionally encrypted SecureString values; managed secret rotation is a different requirement.')
fact(r'CloudHSM|hardware-based key','CloudHSM supplies dedicated cryptographic hardware and customer-controlled key management, not general application policy enforcement.')
fact(r'key rotation|rotate encryption','KMS rotation changes eligible key material while preserving old decryption material; alarms or identity assignments do not perform that rotation.')
fact(r'KMS|encryption keys','KMS manages cryptographic keys and operations for supported service encryption; it does not itself supply a complete authorization, network or backup architecture.')
fact(r'ACM|Certificate Manager|SSL/TLS certificates','ACM manages TLS certificates for supported integrations; certificates must be deployed to TLS endpoints and do not authorize every caller.')
fact(r'TLS|SSL|data in transit|encryption on data transfer','TLS protects a supported connection in transit; endpoints must use and validate it. It does not preserve deleted objects or replicate a database.')
fact(r'MFA Delete','S3 MFA Delete protects certain bucket-versioning and permanent-version-deletion operations; it does not grant or deny ordinary object reads to a list of users.')
fact(r'MFA|Multi-Factor','MFA strengthens interactive authentication. It does not by itself grant IAM permissions, encrypt application data or authenticate an unattended service call.')
fact(r'Identity Center|SSO','IAM Identity Center (formerly AWS SSO) centrally manages workforce access assignments; it is not a data encryption or network routing feature.')
fact(r'\bSTS\b|temporary security credential','STS issues temporary credentials under configured trust and permission relationships; credentials still have to be handled securely.')
fact(r'SCP|Service Control|Organizations','Organizations manages accounts and policies; SCPs constrain member-account permissions but do not grant them or run backups.')
fact(r'bucket polic|S3 Access Points','S3 resource policies and access-point policies scope supported object access. A policy is not encryption and does not automatically revoke every separate applicable allow.')
fact(r'IAM|access control for AWS|permissions that a user|authoriz','IAM expresses who can perform supported actions on resources. Authorization does not by itself encrypt traffic, record all application logs or route network packets.')
fact(r'credentials|access keys','Rotate or remove long-lived keys and prefer temporary role credentials where possible. Rotation limits exposure but cannot guarantee a key is never leaked.')
fact(r'network ACL|subnet level','Network ACLs provide stateless subnet-boundary allow/deny filtering with ordered rules. Return paths must be permitted explicitly; they do not inspect application payloads.')
fact(r'security group|traffic to instances','Security groups provide stateful interface-level allow rules for network traffic. They do not have explicit deny rules or implement application user permissions.')
fact(r'Flow Logs|visibility into.*network traffic','VPC Flow Logs record supported traffic metadata rather than complete payloads or the IAM identity that edited a security policy.')
fact(r'VPC Endpoint|PrivateLink','VPC endpoints provide private paths to supported services. IAM and TLS requirements still apply; interface endpoints and gateway endpoints have different costs and capabilities.')
fact(r'NAT','NAT enables specified outbound address translation for private resources. It is not an inbound authorization system, a global routing service or a data encryption feature.')
fact(r'Internet Gateway','An internet gateway supports VPC internet routing with suitable routes and addressing. It does not encrypt storage or authorize S3 data access.')
fact(r'Elastic IP','An Elastic IP is a public address resource, not an IP filtering policy or a durable application state store.')
fact(r'VPC Peering','VPC peering connects compatible VPCs and is not transitive. It is not an on-premises VPN, encryption feature or application credential service.')
fact(r'Transit Gateway','Transit Gateway interconnects routed networks through a hub. It requires routing/segmentation design and can add attachment and processing costs.')
fact(r'Direct Connect','Direct Connect supplies dedicated private hybrid connectivity, not encryption by default. A private circuit is not a general internet CDN or migration tool by itself.')
fact(r'\bVPN\b|virtual private network routes','VPN establishes encrypted tunnels over a network path. It does not provide DNS, cryptographic key administration or application deployment.')
fact(r'\bVPC\b|isolated network|network configurations|VPC configurations','VPC defines isolated virtual networking, routes and subnet boundaries. It does not automatically make every appliance or application dependency redundant.')
fact(r'WAF|SQL injection|XSS|web exploit|application.layer|geolocation rules','WAF applies HTTP request rules on supported web endpoints, including supported attack-pattern and geographic matching. It is not a blanket control for every AWS resource or all protocols.')
fact(r'Security Hub|security alerts|security findings','Security Hub aggregates supported security findings and posture checks; it does not replace all source detectors or automatically remediate every issue.')
fact(r'GuardDuty|suspicious activity|security incidents','GuardDuty detects threats using supported telemetry and publishes findings. Response actions need a separately configured investigation/remediation workflow.')
fact(r'Inspector|security vulnerabilities','Inspector assesses supported software vulnerabilities and exposure. It does not replace request filtering, packet telemetry or general backup management.')
fact(r'CloudTrail|logs API calls|API activity|API requests|governance, compliance|security auditing','CloudTrail records supported account API activity under configured event coverage. It is not ordinary application performance monitoring or a full packet capture.')
fact(r'\bConfig\b|resource inventory|configuration history|compliance checks','Config records supported resource configurations and evaluates rules; configured remediation is separate. It is detective rather than a universal preventive permission firewall.')
fact(r'Logs Insights|analyze and query log|analyzing log data','CloudWatch Logs Insights queries collected log events. It needs existing logs and does not replace a streaming computation engine or perform backups.')
fact(r'CloudWatch Logs','CloudWatch Logs collects logs and supports query/metric integrations; storing logs alone does not perform every analytical or recovery function.')
fact(r'CloudWatch|monitor and log|monitoring and logging|alarms|performance metrics|application monitoring|monitor application|monitor network traffic','Metrics, logs and alarms observe behavior and can trigger configured actions. Monitoring alone does not scale resources, enforce identity permissions, encrypt data or create replicas.')
fact(r'X-Ray','X-Ray provides distributed tracing for instrumented requests. It complements metrics and logs but is not a database, router or cost billing report.')
fact(r'S3 Intelligent.Tiering|automatically moving data between access tiers','Intelligent-Tiering moves eligible S3 objects between access tiers based on observed access; optional archival tiers require configuration and have restore trade-offs.')
fact(r'Deep Archive','S3 Glacier Deep Archive targets very cold long-duration retention and needs asynchronous restoration. Minimum duration and retrieval charges matter; it is not for immediate reads.')
fact(r'One Zone.IA','S3 One Zone-IA has millisecond retrieval but stores data in one AZ. Use it only when that resilience trade-off and retrieval/minimum-duration charges fit.')
fact(r'Standard.IA','S3 Standard-IA combines infrequent-access pricing with multi-AZ storage and millisecond retrieval, with retrieval and minimum-duration charges.')
fact(r'Glacier','The Glacier storage classes target archival patterns with class-specific retrieval behavior. The generic name does not specify Instant, Flexible or Deep Archive performance/costs.')
fact(r'S3 Standard','S3 Standard targets frequent object access with regional multi-AZ redundancy. It is not a shared POSIX file interface or a block volume.')
fact(r'Transfer Acceleration','S3 Transfer Acceleration can speed suitable long-distance S3 transfers through edge-network paths; it is not a viewer content cache or a universal latency guarantee.')
fact(r'Cross.Region Replication|S3 Replication','S3 replication copies eligible objects under configured rules and permissions. Cross-Region copies improve geographic recovery, but replication is asynchronous and is not a historical backup by itself.')
fact(r'Object Lock','Object Lock prevents deletion/overwriting of retained protected object versions under the selected mode. It is not the feature that creates every version or an access-frequency optimizer.')
fact(r'versioning|retain multiple versions','Versioning preserves object versions to recover overwritten/deleted data. It does not encrypt bytes, lower transfer costs or create cross-Region copies by itself.')
fact(r'Lifecycle|archiving|manage the lifecycle','S3 lifecycle rules manage transitions and expiration using configured rules such as object age. They do not by themselves monitor every object\'s access frequency.')
fact(r'Event Notifications','S3 event notifications notify supported destinations about configured object events. They do not choose a storage class or automatically preserve historical versions.')
fact(r'Storage Class Analysis','S3 storage class analysis informs access-pattern decisions; it is not by itself the lifecycle action that moves objects.')
fact(r'\bS3\b|unstructured data|object storage','S3 is durable object storage. It is not a drop-in relational database, block volume, shared NFS file system or general execution engine.')
fact(r'FSx for Lustre|Lustre|HPC','FSx for Lustre provides a parallel file system for high-throughput computing workloads. It is not the SMB file service for unchanged Windows clients.')
fact(r'FSx for Windows|Windows.based file','FSx for Windows File Server provides managed SMB file storage and Windows integration; its interface differs from EFS NFS and S3 object APIs.')
fact(r'\bFSx\b','FSx supplies purpose-built managed file systems. The selected engine/protocol and deployment type determine performance and availability.')
fact(r'\bEFS\b|file system for Linux','EFS provides shared NFS storage for supported clients. Regional and One Zone durability/availability differ; it does not provide a native Windows SMB service.')
fact(r'Snapshot Manager','The named Amazon EBS Snapshot Manager is not the official Data Lifecycle Manager feature. DLM manages lifecycle for snapshots under its policies, not arbitrary unused snapshots automatically.')
fact(r'EBS|snapshots of instances|Database Snapshots','EBS is persistent AZ-scoped block storage and snapshots are recovery points. Snapshot scheduling/copying is a separate configured operation, not a continuously running database replica.')
fact(r'Storage Gateway','Storage Gateway exposes hybrid file, volume or tape interfaces backed by AWS storage. It is not a universal cloud database or automatic failover service for arbitrary on-premises applications.')
fact(r'\bBackup\b|backup and restore|data backup','Backups supply recovery points and supported restore workflows. Restoration takes time; backup retention does not provide immediate live failover or replace every data replication design.')
fact(r'CloudFront|edge locations|edge caching|content delivery|content closer','CloudFront delivers and caches supported HTTP content at edge locations. It does not deploy regional compute, optimize SQL or provide arbitrary raw TCP acceleration.')
fact(r'Global Accelerator|healthy endpoint|nearest AWS region','Global Accelerator optimizes supported global network paths to configured healthy endpoints. It is not an application deployment service, a content cache or a guarantee of the geographically nearest Region.')
fact(r'latency.based|lowest latency','Latency routing selects configured regional records using latency measurements; it is different from geometric proximity, weighted traffic allocation and primary/secondary failover.')
fact(r'Weighted|specified weight','Weighted routing selects records according to configured relative weights; equal distribution is only one possible weight configuration.')
fact(r'Health Checks|unhealthy endpoints|DNS failover','Health checks can influence configured failover or other health-aware routing. They do not provide database consistency or resource capacity themselves, and DNS caching affects recovery speed.')
fact(r'Route 53|DNS policies|DNS records|Traffic Policies|geographic location of users','Route 53 provides authoritative DNS and supported routing policies. Each policy has distinct selection rules; DNS routing does not provision application copies or replicate data.')
fact(r'Cross.Zone','Cross-zone load balancing extends supported load distribution across AZ target sets according to configuration. It is not an authentication or storage replication feature.')
fact(r'SSL Termination','TLS termination decrypts a supported client connection at the load balancer. It is a separate capability from distributing targets across AZs.')
fact(r'Session Persistence','Session persistence favors routing a client to a particular target. It is not the mechanism that expands traffic distribution across zones.')
fact(r'Load Balanc|Load Balancer|load between|Distribution of incoming|Distribute incoming|Traffic Distribution','Load balancers route requests among configured healthy targets. They do not change the EC2 instance count, replicate data or themselves guarantee redundancy without healthy alternatives.')
fact(r'Spot|unused.*EC2|unused capacity','Spot uses spare EC2 capacity at a discount with interruption and availability trade-offs. Variable demand alone is not proof that a workload tolerates interruption.')
fact(r'Savings Plans','Savings Plans discount eligible committed hourly usage. Scope and flexibility differ from Reserved Instances; unused commitments still cost money and a purchase plan does not scale instance count.')
fact(r'Reserved Instances|commitment discount','Reserved Instances discount eligible matching instance usage over a commitment term. They do not automatically scale capacity or outperform every Savings Plan/Spot option for every workload.')
fact(r'On.Demand','On-Demand uses provisioned capacity without a long-term discount commitment. It fits changing demand when interruption risk is unacceptable, but does not guarantee unlimited immediately available capacity.')
fact(r'Dedicated Hosts|dedicated hardware','Dedicated Hosts provide physical host allocation for supported control/licensing needs. They are not automatically the cheapest burst compute or a guarantee of application HA.')
fact(r'Scaling Policies|Auto Scaling|adjusting the number|adjust the number|adjusting the size|resource scaling|scaling resources|Scale Amazon EC2','EC2 Auto Scaling changes and maintains group instance capacity using configured policies. It does not simply resize live instances vertically, act as a load balancer or operate one group across Regions.')
fact(r'Scheduled Actions','Scheduled actions change configured capacity at specified times. They are useful for known patterns but differ from demand-metric-driven policies.')
fact(r'Launch Configuration','A launch definition supplies instance creation settings, not a demand signal or replication strategy. Modern EC2 Auto Scaling designs use launch templates.')
fact(r'Instance Refresh','Instance refresh rolls replacements through an Auto Scaling group. It is not the ordinary policy deciding how many instances demand requires.')
fact(r'Placement Groups','Placement groups affect instance placement strategies. Their types have different failure/performance trade-offs and do not universally create a multi-AZ application.')
fact(r'\bC Series\b','EC2 C families are compute optimized for CPU-intensive workloads.')
fact(r'\bR Series\b','EC2 R families are memory optimized for large working sets; they are not the general default for a CPU-bound workload.')
fact(r'\bM Series\b','EC2 M families balance compute, memory and network resources for general-purpose workloads.')
fact(r'\bT Series\b','EC2 T families provide burstable performance and credit-based behavior, which differs from sustained compute-optimized demand.')
fact(r'CloudFormation|Infrastructure as Code|infrastructure provisioning','CloudFormation provisions and updates declared resource stacks from templates. A template must encode the desired resilience; infrastructure automation is not automatically a database backup or code delivery pipeline.')
fact(r'CodeDeploy|application deployments|automating deployments|deployment of applications','CodeDeploy automates supported application code deployments. It does not generally define every infrastructure resource and is outside the listed SAA-C03 scope.')
fact(r'CodePipeline','CodePipeline coordinates delivery stages. It invokes deployment/build actions but is not itself an entire infrastructure or data recovery design.')
fact(r'CodeBuild','CodeBuild executes build/test jobs; it is not the service that supplies a database, global routing or backup architecture.')
fact(r'OpsWorks','OpsWorks is a legacy configuration-management service family, not the recommended current general-purpose infrastructure-as-code choice.')
fact(r'Beanstalk|application versions','Elastic Beanstalk manages supported application environments and deployments. HA needs an appropriate multi-instance load-balanced configuration; a managed single instance remains a failure point.')
fact(r'Fargate','Fargate supplies serverless container capacity for supported ECS/EKS configurations. It is not a stand-alone Kubernetes control plane and application service scaling remains a configured policy.')
fact(r'\bEKS\b|Kubernetes','EKS supplies a managed Kubernetes control plane. Worker capacity, networking, workload placement and scaling still need appropriate configuration.')
fact(r'\bECS\b|Docker containers|containerized applications','ECS schedules container services and tasks. The choice of Fargate or EC2 capacity affects host management; generic Docker packaging alone does not distinguish ECS from Kubernetes.')
fact(r'Lambda Pricing','Lambda pricing is the billing model, not a separate cost-management service that automatically optimizes functions.')
fact(r'Lambda Cost Optimization','Lambda cost optimization is a practice, not the standalone service named by this option. Use measured duration, memory, concurrency and billing evidence.')
fact(r'Lambda|serverless code|serverless applications','Lambda executes bounded event-driven functions with automatic concurrency scaling subject to limits. It is not persistent storage, general container orchestration or a universal substitute for long-running server workloads.')
fact(r'\bEC2\b|virtual private servers','EC2 supplies virtual machine instances. Host administration, application deployment and capacity management remain separate concerns from the instance service itself.')
fact(r'Multi.Master','The legacy Aurora Multi-Master wording is not a current general solution for synchronous standby failover; use current supported database deployment models.')
fact(r'Global Tables|fully replicated tables','DynamoDB global tables provide configured multi-Region replicas. Application routing and the selected consistency/conflict model still matter; they are not a recovery mechanism for every database engine.')
fact(r'Read Replica','Read replicas offload eligible reads and typically replicate asynchronously. They do not increase primary write throughput and promotion/recovery is different from synchronous standby failover.')
fact(r'Multi.AZ|synchronous replication','Conventional RDS Multi-AZ DB instances use a synchronous standby in another AZ for managed failover. The single standby is not readable and Multi-AZ does not mean cross-Region recovery.')
fact(r'Global Database|multi.region, low.latency read and write','Aurora Global Database replicates from a primary write Region to secondary clusters. Secondary reads can be local; write forwarding still runs writes at the primary rather than adding independent regional writers.')
fact(r'Aurora','Aurora is a managed MySQL/PostgreSQL-compatible relational database with distributed cluster storage. Global replication and Serverless capacity are distinct optional designs, not automatic properties of every deployment.')
fact(r'DynamoDB Streams','DynamoDB Streams publishes item change records for processing; it is not directly a provisioned read-capacity increase or synchronous RDS failover feature.')
fact(r'read capacity|write throughput units','Provisioned read/write capacity affects DynamoDB throughput for the relevant operation type. Extra write capacity does not fix insufficient read capacity, and vice versa.')
fact(r'DynamoDB','DynamoDB is a managed key-value/document database with capacity modes and indexing choices. It is not a relational SQL engine or a general shared file service.')
fact(r'ElastiCache|in.memory','ElastiCache supplies managed in-memory data stores. Caching can offload repeated reads but requires consistency, expiration and authoritative-data decisions.')
fact(r'RDS|MySQL|PostgreSQL','RDS manages supported relational database engines with optional HA and read-scaling configurations. Native backups, standby failover and read replicas are different mechanisms.')
fact(r'Redshift|data warehous','Redshift targets analytical SQL warehouses. Serverless capacity and provisioned-cluster management differ; it is not a general low-latency key-value transaction store.')
fact(r'Athena','Athena executes supported SQL queries over data such as S3 objects using managed query infrastructure. It is not the durable object storage layer or a live message queue.')
fact(r'Lake Formation','Lake Formation governs data-lake access and integrates with catalog/storage services. S3 remains the underlying object storage; governance alone is not the entire data lake.')
fact(r'Glue','Glue supplies managed data integration, ETL and catalog capabilities. It is not general virtual-server capacity, application load balancing or a drop-in operational database.')
fact(r'DataSync','DataSync automates supported online storage transfers, with network, compatibility and scheduling requirements. It is not an offline shipped device or a relational change-capture engine.')
fact(r'Transfer Family','Transfer Family supports managed file-transfer protocol endpoints. It is not universally cheaper than bulk online synchronization or physical offline transfer.')
fact(r'Database Migration|\bDMS\b','DMS migrates supported database data with full load and change capture. Schema compatibility, network access and final cutover still need design.')
fact(r'Data Pipeline','Data Pipeline is a legacy workflow/data movement offering; it is not the direct managed database change-capture migration service or a cache.')
fact(r'Data Firehose','Data Firehose buffers and delivers streaming records to supported destinations with optional transformations. Delivery is different from arbitrary stateful real-time analytical processing.')
fact(r'Kinesis Data Analytics','Legacy Kinesis Data Analytics naming must distinguish the renamed Flink service from the discontinued SQL Applications service. Streaming compute differs from stream ingestion alone.')
fact(r'Kinesis|real.time data streaming','Kinesis Data Streams ingests and retains records for consumer applications. Analytics computation must be supplied by a consumer or processing service.')
fact(r'QuickSight|Amazon Quick','Amazon Quick/QuickSight provides business intelligence and visualization. It is not the underlying low-latency stream ingestion service.')
fact(r'SageMaker','SageMaker AI provides managed ML lifecycle and deployment capabilities rather than general data integration, DNS or backup orchestration.')
fact(r'\bEMR\b','EMR provides managed big-data processing frameworks. Cluster/serverless processing models differ from an operational database or authoritative S3 storage.')
fact(r'\bSQS\b','SQS buffers durable messages for consuming workers; it is a queue rather than pub-sub fan-out to every subscriber. Delivery and ordering depend on queue type.')
fact(r'\bSNS\b','SNS provides pub-sub distribution to subscriptions. A worker queue such as SQS adds independent durable consumer buffering and retry handling.')
fact(r'Step Functions','Step Functions orchestrates workflow states and service tasks. It does not directly provide a general message backlog, file system or packet route.')
fact(r'Batch','Batch schedules batch computing jobs using configured compute resources. It is not the same as a continuously running web-service orchestrator.')
fact(r'Snowball','Snowball historically used shipped devices for offline bulk transfers. New-customer availability changed; migration design must consider current availability, bandwidth and timelines.')
fact(r'AppSync','AppSync manages supported GraphQL APIs. API Gateway supplies other API front-door models, so an unspecified API-management question may admit both.')
fact(r'App Mesh','App Mesh is service-mesh networking, not an authoritative DNS service. It is not the service asked for by a DNS requirement.')
fact(r'Cost Allocation Tags','Activated cost allocation tags attach project/department attribution to supported billing records. Creating ordinary resource tags alone is not sufficient for cost reporting.')
fact(r'Tag Editor','Tag Editor applies supported resource tags in bulk; billing activation of eligible cost allocation tags is a separate step.')
fact(r'Pricing Calculator','Pricing Calculator estimates proposed service usage costs from inputs before deployment; it does not report the actual complete billing history.')
fact(r'Cost Explorer','Cost Explorer provides interactive cost/usage analysis, forecasts and supported purchase/rightsizing recommendations. It can overlap with Advisor for optimization but is not a universal auto-remediator.')
fact(r'Cost and Usage Report|billing reports|detailed billing','CUR supplies detailed billing/usage exports for downstream analysis. Interactive views and threshold actions are separate cost-management capabilities.')
fact(r'Budgets','Budgets monitors configured spending/usage thresholds and can send alerts or configured supported actions. It is not a universal instantaneous hard spending cap.')
fact(r'Trusted Advisor','Trusted Advisor provides supported best-practice and cost checks. It recommends actions rather than automatically eliminating every unused resource.')
fact(r'Data Transfer','Data transfer describes a billing/network activity rather than a standalone universal cost-saving service with this name.')
fact(r'Systems Manager','Systems Manager supports fleet operations and configured automation. It can run custom workflows but is not a built-in universal snapshot optimizer or every security enforcement mechanism.')
fact(r'Free Tier','Free Tier eligibility and limits depend on the offer and account conditions; it is not a committed baseline purchase discount.')
fact(r'guaranteed availability','No capacity purchase choice in this context guarantees unlimited continuously available application capacity. Spot in particular can be reclaimed.')
fact(r'Higher computing capacity','An instance\'s configured type determines resources; a discounted purchase model alone does not add CPU or memory.')
fact(r'Increased storage capacity|increase storage capacity','Capacity growth is a different property from replication, availability, version retention or object access control.')
fact(r'write throughput','Increasing write throughput is distinct from offloading reads, key encryption or a standby failover mechanism.')
fact(r'query performance|database queries','Query optimization concerns execution plans, capacity, indexing or caching; a content delivery or DNS service does not optimize database SQL.')
fact(r'data consistency','Consistency depends on database replication and read/write semantics. DNS checks and compute scaling do not establish database consistency.')
fact(r'data replication|Replication|replicated','Replication maintains copies under a specific mechanism and consistency model. It is not interchangeable with backups, encryption or any one service\'s native storage durability.')
fact(r'Durability|fault tolerance|High availability|availability and data durability','Durability and availability are architecture outcomes, not switches that apply identically to every service/class. Redundant failure domains and suitable recovery mechanisms must be specified.')
fact(r'Encryption|Data encryption at rest|encrypt data','Encryption protects data confidentiality under a key/transport model. It does not itself recover deleted objects, distribute traffic, scale capacity or prove the caller is authorized.')
fact(r'cost|billing|data transfer charges','A billing or cost property depends on the actual resource, usage and pricing model. It does not alone specify a networking, security or availability mechanism.')
fact(r'manage application dependencies|source code repository','Dependency/repository management is a separate lifecycle function from traffic distribution and infrastructure backup.')
fact(r'Performance Insights','Database performance diagnostics help identify workload issues; they do not create an HA standby or automatically scale all database resources.')
fact(r'Automated Backups|Automatic backups','Automated backups preserve supported recovery points for restoration. They do not supply immediate application failover, increase active query capacity or replace replication.')
fact(r'Increase the instance type','A larger instance can improve available compute or memory when that is the bottleneck, but does not guarantee better performance for every access pattern.')
fact(r'Persistent storage of logs','Persisting logs is an observability/storage concern rather than the defining execution or elasticity characteristic of a compute service.')
fact(r'cloud resources|cloud resource','Resource management scope must be specified: identity permissions, network paths, application compute and storage recovery use different mechanisms.')

fact(r'encrypt|confidentiality','Stored-data encryption and TLS protect different data paths; encryption does not replace replication, query capacity or traffic distribution.')
fact(r'network access|network security|security policies|Built.in security|data security|security for your applications','Security policy and access-control configuration address who or what may access a resource. They are distinct from the particular performance, replication or compute mechanism under discussion.')
fact(r'archive','Archival storage concerns long retention and retrieval trade-offs. Automatic movement into an archive requires an explicit lifecycle or access-tier policy.')
fact(r'\bVPCs\b|private cloud|network configuration','Network isolation and routing are distinct from managed compute, encryption and resource authorization; no isolated network alone supplies all those properties.')
fact(r'database backups','Database backups create recovery points for later restore. They do not supply a live reader or active traffic routing.')
fact(r'scaling database|scaling based on traffic','Capacity scaling adjusts an explicitly supported resource under configured policies. It is different from storage replication and does not universally imply automated scaling for every database.')
fact(r'Dedicated instance|dedicated instances','Dedicated/assigned compute capacity is different from serverless invocation scaling and from the storage or replication mechanism that provides database durability.')
fact(r'Deploy instances in multiple Availability Zones','Multi-AZ placement reduces an AZ failure dependency when sufficient redundant capacity and dependencies are present. It does not alone route requests or create regional copies.')
fact(r'Deploy instances in multiple regions','Multiple Regions can support disaster recovery, but need data replication, workload deployment and traffic routing. Placement alone is not a complete HA design.')
fact(r'Deploy instances in a single region','A single Region can still span AZs; this statement alone does not establish redundant capacity, request distribution or protection from a regional outage.')
fact(r'deployment|deploy applications|Deploying scalable|provisioning|configuration of infrastructure','Application deployment changes executable application versions; infrastructure provisioning changes declared resources. Neither alone is a network-routing or database-replication mechanism.')
fact(r'balance traffic|Distribute traffic evenly','Load balancing selects among targets; DNS weighting can approximate weighted selection. Even distribution is not the definition of every routing policy.')
fact(r'disaster recovery','Disaster recovery requires recoverable data, recreateable infrastructure and tested traffic recovery against RPO/RTO. A service name alone is not a complete recovery strategy.')
fact(r'log|Health Monitoring|Monitoring AWS|Monitors and manages|monitor AWS|Simplified monitoring|Manage the performance','Observability collects evidence and helps diagnose faults. Alarms or dashboards need configured actions to change capacity, enforce access or restore data.')
fact(r'analytics|processing capabilities','Data analytics needs a query/processing engine and suitable data sources. A network path, storage class or traffic distribution feature does not perform arbitrary analytical computation.')
fact(r'underlying server infrastructure','Customer administration of EC2 worker hosts is different from Fargate, where AWS manages the underlying hosts. Container resource and application configuration still remain customer responsibilities.')
fact(r'Managed Databases|Simplified database management','A managed database automates selected database operations, not application deployment, resource template management or every workload storage/compute design.')
fact(r'network performance|Optimize network traffic|Reduced latency|object retrieval speed','Network latency optimization concerns placement, routing or caching of a specified path. It is different from version preservation, encryption and database capacity.')
fact(r'Increased storage performance','Higher storage performance depends on storage service configuration and workload needs; a standby availability mechanism is not a general IOPS scaling feature.')
fact(r'Storing large volumes','Storing data needs a durable storage service and appropriate interface. A function execution or orchestration service is not itself the persistent storage layer.')

fact(r'endpoint health','Health-aware routing uses configured health evidence to avoid unhealthy destinations. This does not itself replicate data, provision capacity or define every DNS policy.')
fact(r'monitors network traffic','Network monitoring observes traffic metadata or packets through a specific telemetry mechanism. It is not encryption, key management or complete API audit history.')
fact(r'security for data at rest','At-rest encryption and authorization protect stored data under a defined key/access model. A transfer speed or content delivery feature does not implement every stored-data security requirement.')
fact(r'consistent performance for web servers','Predictable server performance depends on suitable capacity and workload configuration. Spot reclamation risk cannot be treated as a guarantee of steady availability.')
fact(r'API Gateway','API Gateway provides managed front doors for supported REST, HTTP and WebSocket APIs with configured authorization and traffic controls. AppSync provides a different GraphQL API model.')
fact(r'availability zones','Multiple AZs reduce reliance on a single AZ if the application and dependencies have sufficient redundant capacity. They do not automatically provide cross-Region recovery.')
fact(r'performance and availability|availability and performance','Application performance and availability require a specified mechanism: healthy endpoint routing, capacity, replication and caching address different constraints. A generic claim does not define a unique service.')
fact(r'read performance','Read capacity, read replicas and caching address different read-path bottlenecks. Their usefulness depends on access patterns, consistency and the constrained resource.')
def option_fact(text):
    for pattern,value in FACTS:
        if pattern.search(text): return value
    raise ValueError('Missing reviewed option fact: '+text)
